WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.977
Look at the two sides of the diagram.

2
00:00:02.117 --> 00:00:05.806
An individual contributor completes a reservation accurately.

3
00:00:05.946 --> 00:00:11.996
A manager makes sure the team has clear instructions, appropriate coverage, and a way to notice problems.

4
00:00:12.136 --> 00:00:13.824
Both contributions matter.

5
00:00:13.964 --> 00:00:19.127
Management changes your responsibilities; it does not make other careers less valuable.

6
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:24.591
Chapter twenty-one describes managerial work as varied, brief, and frequently interrupted.

7
00:00:24.731 --> 00:00:29.467
Avery may move from a patron complaint to a training question to a staffing decision.

8
00:00:29.607 --> 00:00:33.087
Being busy is not itself evidence of effective management.

9
00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:37.204
The question is whether attention goes where it helps the service and team.

10
00:00:37.344 --> 00:00:44.430
Managers also face ambiguity: information is incomplete, and reasonable people may prefer different options.

11
00:00:44.571 --> 00:00:47.854
Avery cannot promise a perfect booking system immediately.

12
00:00:47.994 --> 00:00:54.015
A useful decision explains the goal, the constraints, and what evidence would lead to a change of course.

13
00:00:54.155 --> 00:00:59.826
Technical skills support specific tasks; human relations skills help colleagues work together.

14
00:00:59.966 --> 00:01:04.950
Conceptual skills connect individual decisions to the library's larger purpose.

15
00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.225
Developing all three takes practice.

16
00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:12.807
In the activity, choose the response that builds the team's ability to handle recurring problems.
